WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
Speaker 1: Welcome to the show, everybody, lots&nbsp;
of big news to talk about, including some really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:10.080
important pharmaceutical developments, as we might&nbsp;
call them. Merck is now pushing into the Emergency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:19.520
Use Authorization Request Phase, a so-called COVID&nbsp;
pill that they say based on their trials reduces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:27.360
the risk of hospitalization and death by about&nbsp;
50 percent. Now my view on this has been that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:33.920
politics aside, having an effective treatment&nbsp;
in addition to a vaccine is a really great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:38.720
thing. There's a lot of people thinking about&nbsp;
this in the context of the unvaccinated. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:44.720
now the unvaccinated don't have to worry because&nbsp;
they can take a pill if they get the virus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.680
and it cuts risk of hospitalization and death by&nbsp;
50 percent. There may be people that are going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:55.200
think about it that way, but not me. Let's talk&nbsp;
about the developments and then we will go from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:01.520
there. Merck says COVID pill cuts risk of death&nbsp;
and hospitalization in a potential leap forward in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:08.080
the global fight against the pandemic. Drugmaker&nbsp;
Merck said Friday that its experimental pill for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.680
people sick with COVID reduced hospitalizations&nbsp;
and deaths by half. If cleared by regulators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:19.200
it would be the first pill shown to treat&nbsp;
COVID 19, adding a whole new easy to use weapon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:25.520
to an arsenal that already includes the vaccine.&nbsp;
The company said it will soon ask health officials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:30.400
in the U.S. and around the world to authorize&nbsp;
the pills, use a decision from the U.S. Food and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.960
Drug Administration could come within weeks. After&nbsp;
that, all other COVID treatments authorized in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:42.080
U.S. require an IV or an injection. A pill taken&nbsp;
at home, by contrast, would ease pressure pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:49.440
on hospitals and could also help curb outbreaks&nbsp;
in poorer and more remote corners of the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:54.560
that don't have access to the more expensive&nbsp;
infusion therapies. This would allow us to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:00.800
treat many more people much more quickly, and we&nbsp;
trust much less expensively. Merck and its partner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:06.320
Ridgeback Biotherapeutics as early, said early&nbsp;
results showed patients who received the drug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:12.880
molnupiravir within five days of COVID symptoms&nbsp;
had about half the rate of hospitalization and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:20.320
death as those who received a dummy pill the&nbsp;
placebo. The study tracked 775 adults with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:25.360
mild to moderate COVID, considered high risk for&nbsp;
disease because of health problems like obesity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.440
diabetes or heart disease. The results have&nbsp;
not yet been reviewed by outside experts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:36.080
The usual procedure for vetting new medical&nbsp;
research among patients taking molnupiravir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.480
seven point three percent were either&nbsp;
hospitalized or died at the end of 30 days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:45.120
compared with 14 percent of those getting the&nbsp;
dummy pill. That is a significant reduction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:51.120
after that time period. There were no deaths among&nbsp;
those who received the drug, compared with eight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.600
deaths in the placebo group. This is one of those&nbsp;
cases we've talked about before medical ethics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:03.760
When the test, a medication or procedure&nbsp;
seems to be working so well, it becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:08.960
unethical to keep people on the placebo. And&nbsp;
in fact, that's what they talk about here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:14.960
The results were so strong that an independent&nbsp;
group of medical experts monitoring the trial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:21.120
recommended stopping it early. Executives plan&nbsp;
to submit data to the FDA in the coming days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:27.040
and experts stress the importance of getting&nbsp;
vaccinated anyway. So again, a lot of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:31.120
hearing this and saying, we've been saying&nbsp;
there's going to be a treatment of some kind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:37.040
we don't need to get vaccinated. That's not at all&nbsp;
what this is about. My thinking is the following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:43.520
math. This is a great additional layer in addition&nbsp;
